## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming algorithms with bounded memory and error guarantees. Reservoir sampling is a fundamental technique that demonstrates trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and per-element computational cost.

## Key Concepts
- **Reservoir Sampling**: Maintains a uniform random sample of size S from an infinite stream
- **Unbiased Estimator**: (N/S) * sumS has expectation equal to the true sum
- **Relative Error Bound**: Using Chernoff bounds, P(|X - E[X]| > epsilon * E[X]) <= 2 * exp(-S * epsilon^2 / 3), giving S >= (3/epsilon^2) * ln(2/delta)
- **Time Complexity**: O(1) per element (constant-time random number generation and potential replacement)
- **Space Complexity**: O(S) for storing the reservoir

## Derivation
The probability that any given stream element ends up in the final reservoir is S/N. By linearity of expectation, E[sumS] = S/N * (sum of all N values), so E[(N/S) * sumS] = true sum — the estimator is unbiased. The variance of sumS scales as O(N * M^2 / S), and the relative standard deviation is O(sqrt(N/S)), giving the error bounds above.

## Code Example
```javascript
let S = 100;
let N = 0, sumS = 0;
let sample = [];

function process(value) {
  N++;
  if (sample.length < S) {
    sample.push(value);
    sumS += value;
  } else {
    let j = Math.floor(Math.random() * N);
    if (j < S) {
      sumS += value - sample[j];
      sample[j] = value;
    }
  }
}

function estimateSum() {
  return N === 0 ? 0 : (N / S) * sumS;
}
```

## Worked Example
M=100, B=10, S=50. Stream: [5, 12, 7, 23, 8, ...]. After first batch of 5 values (N=5): reservoir = [5,12,7,23,8], sumS = 55, estimate = (5/5)*55 = 55 (exact). After 100 values with sum=2340: reservoir has 50 values summing to ~1170, estimate = (100/50)*1170 = 2340 (close to true). With S=50 and epsilon=0.1, delta ≈ exp(-50*0.01/3) ≈ 0.85 — increase S to 400 for delta=0.01.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic spatial indices under batched inserts and to reason about amortized bounds, practical thresholds, and rebuild strategies in proximity queries common in ML data pipelines or trading systems.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-grid hashing and locality
- Amortized analysis with batched updates
- Rebuild/rehash trade-offs and density handling
- Space-time trade-offs vs brute force

## Code Example
```javascript
class GridIndex {
  constructor(D){ this.D=D; this.s=D/Math.sqrt(2); this.buckets=new Map(); }
  cell(coord){ return Math.floor(coord/this.s); }
  key(x,y){ const cx = this.cell(x); const cy = this.cell(y); return cx+','+cy; }
  insert(p){ const x=p[0], y=p[1];
    for(let i=-1;i<=1;i++) for(let j=-1;j<=1;j++){
      const k=(Math.floor(x/this.s)+i)+','+(Math.floor(y/this.s)+j);
      const bucket=this.buckets.get(k); if(bucket){ for(const q of bucket){ if(Math.hypot(x-q[0],y-q[1])<=this.D) return true; } }
    }
    const kk=Math.floor(x/this.s)+','+Math.floor(y/this.s);
    if(!this.buckets.has(kk)) this.buckets.set(kk, []);
    this.buckets.get(kk).push(p);
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle deletions or moving points?
- What are worst-case scenarios if density spikes in a single bucket, and how would you mitigate?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of approximate quantiles with streaming data, a common constraint in production systems. It compares simple histograms to exact tracking and asks for concrete complexity and error bounds.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms for quantiles
- Per-element O(1) updates, O(W) queries
- Error bounds from bucket width w

## Code Example
```javascript
function HistQuantile(m, W) {
  const bucket = new Array(W).fill(0);
  this.n = 0; this.m = m; this.W = W;
  const w = Math.ceil(m / W);
  this.update = (x) => { const idx = Math.min(Math.floor(x / w), W-1); bucket[idx]++; this.n++; };
  this.median = () => {
    let target = Math.floor(this.n / 2) + 1;
    let cum = 0; let i = 0; const bw = Math.ceil(m / W);
    for (; i < W; i++) { if (cum + bucket[i] >= target) break; cum += bucket[i]; }
    const L = i * bw; const left = target - cum;
    const est = left <= bucket[i] ? L + (left / bucket[i]) * bw : L + bw;
    return est;
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust for skewed data?  
- How does choosing W affect error vs memory?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design incremental geometry data structures, reason about dominance, and assess batch-update trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Pareto frontier maintenance
- Batch incremental updates
- Monotone frontier properties (x increasing, y decreasing)
- Trade-offs: update time vs rebuild cost

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch: merge batch into frontier
function insertBatch(frontier, batch){
  batch.sort((a,b)=> a.x-b.x);
  // prune intra-batch dominated
  let filtered=[]; let maxY=-Infinity;
  for(let p of batch){ if(p.y>maxY){ filtered.push(p); maxY=p.y; } }
  // merge with frontier (frontier: sorted by x, y strictly decreasing)
  let i=0,j=0; let newFrontier=[];
  while(i<frontier.length || j<filtered.length){
    let a = frontier[i], b = filtered[j];
    if(!a) { newFrontier.push(...filtered.slice(j)); break; }
    if(!b) { newFrontier.push(...frontier.slice(i)); break; }
    if(a.x < b.x){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; }
    else if(b.x < a.x){ newFrontier.push(b); j++; }
    else { // equal x, keep larger y
      if(a.y > b.y){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; j++; }
      else { newFrontier.push(b); i++; j++; }
    }
  }
  // optional prune to maintain y-decreasing property
  return pruneFrontier(newFrontier);
}
```

